He threw the seeds on the ground. Some of them fell on a path. Birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell in rocky places, where there wasn't much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil wasn't deep.
When the sun rose, it scorched the plants. They withered because they had no roots.
Other seeds fell among the thorns; the thorns grew up and choked the seeds.
And some seeds fell on fertile ground, and they yielded a harvest many times over, reaching a hundred, or sixty, or thirty times what was sown.
They have no eyes to see.
